# Brand visibility inside AI assistants — what the evidence supports

As of 2026-09-23. Evidence only; the verdict is the reader's. Each figure carries source kind, evidence tier (1 strongest, 7 weakest) and date. Terms: Glossary.

## The answer

Demand is real but thin and enterprise-heavy; paid placement inside assistants sells on one disclosed engine; no published case shows it causes sales.

1. **Demand.** 8 of 27 segment cells show a spend signal under both strict and loose readings (analyst-derived, 2026-09-23). On the 2026-09-22 read, 6 of 7 spend cells rested on job postings, the weakest spend class. Willingness to pay: unknown in 27 of 27 cells; no buyer discloses a price paid.
2. **Paid inventory.** Four engines sell ads inside AI answers (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-23). One discloses revenue: ChatGPT Ads "$1 billion in annualized revenue run rate" (company-stated, 3, 2026-08-31). Rate cards: 0 of 8 engines (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22).
3. **Size.** No sub-market has a measured size; every dollar size is a forecast (analyst-derived, 6, 2026-09-22).
4. **What movers changed.** Of 108 cases: content 63, tooling 58, paid media 2 (measured-by-us, 2026-09-23).
5. **Where it stops.** 0 causal-design (Gold) cases in ~980 candidates (2026-09-22) and ~1,640 more (2026-09-23), measured-by-us over vendor cases, tier 5. The two highest-tier controlled cases run negative or null.

## What this is

Three sub-markets, kept apart. **Organic recommendation** (also GEO, AEO): the brand appears in AI answers unpaid; bought by brand marketing and SEO owners. **Paid placement**: ad inventory inside AI surfaces; bought by media buyers. **Agentic commerce**: the agent executes checkout; bought by e-commerce and payments teams. Visibility, traffic and sales are separate metrics; visibility change alone is never sales proof.

## Why now

The record holds 54 dated events, 26 at tier 2 (measured-by-us count, 2026-09-23). Among them: Copilot Checkout, "does not take a commission", 2026-01-08; "Claude will remain ad-free", 2026-02-04; ChatGPT Ads US pilot, 2026-02-09 (all company-stated, 3). NerdWallet cards revenue "decreased 24%", citing "AI overviews and LLMs", 2026-02-25; People Inc "63% decline in Google Search referrals over two years", 2026-05-04; UK CMA fair-ranking requirement covering "search generative AI features", 2026-06-17; EU AI Act Art. 50 in force, 2026-08-02; ChatGPT designated VLOSE, 159.1M EU recipients, duties from 2027-01, 2026-08-31; court exhibit, Microsoft "83-93% drops in click-through rates" for publisher domains, 2026-09-17 (all filed, 2). Profound "$180M Series D at $1.8B valuation", 2026-09-15 (vendor-reported, 3). Perplexity is "winding down" its ads "by the end of 2026" (analyst-derived, 5, 2026-02-18).

## How big

- **Organic.** Floor from disclosed ARR: $42.2M–$48.2M + €2.2M, 4 vendors, dated 2025-11 to 2026-05 (filed 2; company-stated 5); an earlier build differs, Appendix. Forecasts, 2034: $17.15B–$32.92B, 1.92× apart, 4 forecasts (analyst-derived, 6, compiled 2026-09-23). Prices published by 14 of 34 vendors, "$20/mo" to "$999/month" self-serve (vendor-reported, 3, 2026-09-22).
- **Paid.** Floor: the $1B run rate above, one engine. Forecasts, 2030: "just over $5 billion" to "over $100 billion", ~20×, scopes differ (analyst-derived, 5, compiled 2026-09-23). Only engine price: OpenAI bid guidance "$3–$5 USD per click" (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22).
- **Agentic.** Floor: unknown — checked OpenAI, Shopify, PayPal, Stripe, Google, Amazon 2026-09-23. Forecasts, 2030: $190B–$5T, 26.3×, 7 forecasts (analyst-derived, 5–6, compiled 2026-09-23); another spread exists, Appendix. Fees: Copilot 0% (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22); OpenAI "a small fee", rate undisclosed (company-stated, 3, stated 2025-09-29).

Reach, company-stated, 3, definitions differ, never summed: ChatGPT "more than 1 billion" weekly active users (2026-08-31); Google AI Mode "1B MAU", AI Overviews 2.5B monthly (2026-05-19); Gemini app 950M monthly (2026-07-22). AI tools held 1.65% of US desktop search events, Jan–Mar 2026 (vendor-reported, 5). Baseline: Google "Search & other" $224,532M, FY2025 (filed, 2). Attention, not dollars: 76% of "more than 200" ad decision-makers name AI answers their No. 1 area of increased focus (analyst-derived, 4, released 2026-09-10).

## Who is already there

**Vendors.** 43 profiled (2026-09-23). Of 34 organic-side names: price disclosed 14, customer count 8, funding 8, filed revenue 2, none broken out to this product (vendor-reported, 2026-09-22). Largest disclosed product line: Semrush "AI products surpassed $38 million in ARR" (filed, 2, 2025-12). Acquired: Semrush by Adobe (2026-04-28), Scrunch by Sitecore (2026-06-03), GoShine by Yext (2026-09-01). Prompt set behind a vendor's score disclosed: 0 of 34 (vendor-reported, 3, 2026-09-22).

**Engines.** Ads inside answers: ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews and AI Mode, Microsoft Copilot, Amazon Rufus; an earlier count on another basis exists. ChatGPT advertisers "tens of thousands" (company-stated, 3, 2026-08-31); panels count 820 to 7,378 (vendor-reported, 5, 2026-07). Four checkout programmes live (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22); protocol fee clauses: 6 of 6 unknown — checked each protocol owner's domain 2026-09-22. Native visibility tools: 0 at OpenAI and Anthropic (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22). Google: "no additional requirements... nor other special optimizations necessary" (company-stated, 3).

**Buyers.** 27 cells: three sub-markets × three verticals × three buyer sizes. Strict reading: spend 8, attention 1, none — checked 17, blank 1. Loose: 8, 1, 18, 0 (analyst-derived, 2026-09-23; earlier reads exist). Spend by sub-market: organic 6, paid 1, agentic 1. On the 2026-09-22 read, 5 of 7 spend cells were enterprise and 1 of 9 SMB cells spent. One cell reads spend to one compiler, attention to another; both stand. Local and multi-location vertical: spend 1, attention 3, none 5. EU country cells: 45, spend 2, none 41 (both analyst-derived, 2026-09-23).

## What the proof shows

- Silver (pre/post with control): rule-1 reading 1 (2026-09-22) plus 1 (2026-09-23); a raw reading exists (vendor-reported, 5). Of the 7 raw Silvers, 2 run negative or null: a filed revenue line and a published replication (filed 2; analyst-derived 4; 2026-09-22).
- 108 transition cases by evidence class, R1 reading: experimental 6, observational 61, action named with outcome unknown 37 (measured-by-us, 2026-09-23); an R2 reading exists.
- Brands confirming a vendor's case on their own pages: 1 of 168 (measured-by-us, 3, 2026-09-23); an earlier count exists. Vendor paid by outcome: 0 yes, 0 no, 12 unknown (vendor-reported, 6, 2026-09-22).
- Paid ads: 0 of 15 results screened carry a control; 10 observational self-reports (company-stated, 5, 2026-09-23).

Negative: People Inc "22% decline in Core Sessions" (filed, 2, 2026-08-03); Chegg subscribers "decline of 40%" (filed, 2, 2025-08-05); HubSpot blog "5 million" fewer visits in thirty days (company-stated, 3, 2026-09-22). Null: 2023-era optimisation levers move citation on "none" of ten engine families (analyst-derived, 4, 2026-09-07). Positive, tier 5, vendor-measured: "+300% more site visits from AI Search" (2026-02-23 to 06-29); citations 48 vs 426 across two test communities (2026-04-11 to 06-10). Conversion: "AI Conversion Now 42% Higher" than non-AI visits (Adobe, vendor-reported, 4, 2026-03); "nearly 50% higher rates than organic search" (Shopify, vendor-reported, 5, Q1 2026); neither per engine.

## What could kill it

Threats to the hypothesis that moving marketing into AI assistants measurably pays, ranked by tier of strongest evidence, then breadth (register, 2026-09-23). Likelihood, impact, mitigation, owner: no source states one.

1. Referral collapse: the court exhibit above (2).
2. Regulation: Art. 50, VLOSE duties (2); AI Overviews ads exclude finance, healthcare (3).
3. Vendor consolidation: Adobe, Yext deals (2).
4. Platform capture: OpenAI "more than 50" measurement partners; Google campaigns auto-eligible, "you can't opt out" (3).
5. No payback evidence: HubSpot loss (3); 0 Gold (5).
6. Measurement opacity: 0 of 34 prompt sets (3); ChatGPT ad presence 0.8%–26% across panels (5).
7. "Do nothing" suffices: Google guidance (3); levers null (4).
8. Engine policy reversal: Claude ad-free (3); Perplexity wind-down (5).
9. Agentic fees undisclosed: 6 of 6 clauses (3).
10. Manipulation and countermeasures: Google spam policy names "manipulate generative AI responses", 2026-08-28 (3).
11. Unsizeable market: 18 figures, 15 forecasts, 0 measured (6); a later count adds forecasts.

## Where the evidence stops

- Load-bearing claims at tier 3 or better, six counts (2026-09-23): 11 of 25 = 44.0%; 11 of 18 = 61.1%; 11 of 22 = 50.0%; 12 of 27 = 44.4%; 12 of 19 = 63.2%; 12 of 24 = 50.0%. None meets the 80% bar; an earlier count exists.
- 32 pre-registered hypotheses: 26 scored (confirmed 11 plus 3 dual-marked, killed 7, unresolved 5 plus the 3 dual); 6 not produced, all needing engine sampling, skipped 2026-09-23.
- Blocked 2026-09-23: indeed.com, emarketer.com, perplexity.ai/hub, live reddit.com. Paywalled: Crunchbase, Gartner, Forrester.
- Price any buyer paid: unknown — checked filings, review sites, vendor pricing pages, case files 2026-09-22.
- Any engine rate card: unknown — checked OpenAI, Google, Microsoft, Amazon ad pages 2026-09-22.
- Per-engine conversion: unknown — checked Adobe, Salesforce, Shopify 2026-09-22.
- Whether any posting was filled: unknown — checked LinkedIn 2026-09-23.
- 41 figures are carried twice in the record; those touched here are paired in Appendix A.

## What this asks of you

No decision is requested; no lean is attached. The record leaves five choices to the reader: which sub-market a reading rests on; which reading governs where two stand (Silver raw or rule-1, strict or loose, R1 or R2); whether own engine sampling reopens, and from which vantage; whether credential-gated sources (Crunchbase, PitchBook, Gartner, Forrester, Bloomberg, EMARKETER, Statista) enter scope; whether this question returns to scope: "which of the gaps above, if any, is reachable by a team whose cost advantage is engineering rather than distribution?"
